{
 "schema_version": 1,
 "name": "Paul trap (r-z, hyperbolic) \u2014 Finnigan ITMS commercial geometry, He cooling",
 "notes": "3-D quadrupole ion trap, cylindrically symmetric, solved in r-z. Geometry after the Finnigan ITMS/ITD as documented in the two trap papers: ring inner radius r0 = 10.00 mm; endcap apex at z0 = 7.83 mm -- the COMMERCIAL geometry, i.e. endcaps moved outward from the theoretical 7.07 mm WITHOUT reshaping, so the surfaces still follow the z0 = 7.07 hyperbolae (ring r^2-2z^2 = r0^2, endcap r^2-2z^2 = -2z0^2). z0/r0 = 0.783 matches the experimentally optimal moved-HIT value and Krylov's computed optimum of 0.78. One 0.5 mm ejection hole per endcap: the holes are the reason the field goes sub-linear near the endcaps and are what the chemical mass shift comes from -- a trap modelled without them cannot show it. Operating point: 455 V (0-pk) at 1 MHz gives q_z = 0.40 for m/z 100 via q_z = 8eV/(m(r0^2+2z0^2)w^2); ions cool in 0.02 Torr helium. Compare against the theoretical z0 = 7.07 geometry in notebook 06.",
